complete(identifier,dict(
 title='Successor-invariant first-order model checking on nowhere dense graphs',status='source_open',criterion='resources',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For every effectively nowhere dense class \(\mathcal C\) of finite simple undirected graphs, do there exist a deterministic algorithm \(A_{\mathcal C}\), a total computable function \(f_{\mathcal C}:\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z_{\ge1}\), and an integer constant \(c_{\mathcal C}\ge1\) such that, given a graph \(G\in\mathcal C\) and a successor-invariant first-order sentence \(\varphi\) in the signature \(\{E,S\}\), the algorithm decides
\[
(G,S_G)\models\varphi
\]
in at most
\[
f_{\mathcal C}(|\varphi|)(L+2)^{c_{\mathcal C}}
\]
bit operations? Here \(S_G\) is any successor relation on the vertices, \(L\) is the full input encoding length, and both effectiveness and successor invariance are defined below. The exponent may depend on the graph class but not on the sentence or graph.''',
 definitions=r'''A graph has a finite vertex set \([n]\), possibly empty, with an irreflexive symmetric edge relation \(E\). Its vertices and edges are explicitly listed using binary indices. There are no additional labels, numerical weights or relation symbols. A graph class is any collection closed under isomorphism; closure under taking subgraphs is not additionally required.

For a nonnegative integer \(r\), a graph \(F\) is a depth-\(r\) minor of \(G\) if there are pairwise disjoint nonempty vertex sets \((B_v)_{v\in V(F)}\) such that every \(G[B_v]\) is connected and has a center from which every vertex of \(B_v\) is at graph distance at most \(r\) within \(G[B_v]\), and, for each edge \(\{v,w\}\) of \(F\), some edge of \(G\) connects \(B_v\) to \(B_w\). Extra edges are permitted. The complete graph \(K_m\) has all pairs of distinct vertices as edges. The class \(\mathcal C\) is effectively nowhere dense if there is a total computable function \(b:\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z_{\ge1}\) such that \(K_{b(r)}\) is not a depth-\(r\) minor of any \(G\in\mathcal C\), for every \(r\ge0\). One program computing \(b\) must work for all depths. A fixed algorithm for the class may depend on this witness; the witness is not part of each graph input.

A successor relation on an \(n\)-element vertex set is
\[
S_G=\{(v_i,v_{i+1}):1\le i<n\}
\]
for some enumeration \(v_1,\ldots,v_n\) of all its vertices. It is a directed spanning path independent of the graph edges, with no edge returning from the last vertex to the first. On an empty or singleton set it is empty. It is not the transitive ordering relation.

A first-order sentence is built from equality and the binary predicates \(E,S\), Boolean connectives and quantification over individual vertices; it has no free variables, second-order quantifiers, fixed-point operators or numerical arithmetic. Its size \(|\varphi|\) is its finite binary syntactic encoding length, including variable identifiers. It is successor-invariant if, on every finite simple undirected graph \(G\), the truth value of \((G,S_G)\models\varphi\) is the same for every successor relation on that graph. This promise is global over finite graphs, not only over the particular input class.

The input consists of the graph and sentence. A successor relation need not be supplied, and the algorithm may choose one. Membership in \(\mathcal C\) and successor invariance of \(\varphi\) are promises. The algorithm is not asked to test either promise or to decide which sentences are invariant. No correctness or time guarantee is imposed outside the promises. Ordinary graph and formula encoding choices that are polynomially interconvertible give the same fixed-parameter target.

The algorithm and the function \(f_{\mathcal C}\) are fixed before the graph and sentence vary. Total computability of this function means one finite program returns its value on every nonnegative integer, with no bound on that program's running time. An algorithm with an input-size exponent depending on \(|\varphi|\) does not qualify. Neither an expressive-power collapse to ordinary first-order logic nor a uniform procedure recognizing graph classes is required. A theorem only for classes of bounded expansion, or a theorem for full order invariance instead of successor invariance, does not answer this selected question.''',
 answer_criterion='Give a complete mathematically correct Lean-checked proof or refutation of the displayed statement for every effectively nowhere dense graph class. A positive answer must supply the class-dependent uniform algorithms, computable parameter functions and fixed input-size exponents. A negative answer must establish failure for a class satisfying the stated effective minor-exclusion condition; an unproved parameterized-complexity assumption must not be silently presented as an unconditional refutation.',
 importance=dict(score=85,method='editorial',reason='This tests whether access to an arbitrary but invariant successor preserves the main sparse-graph tractability frontier of first-order logic. A positive result would extend a central algorithmic meta-theorem; a separation would expose an algorithmic cost of invariant auxiliary structure.'),
 why='An auxiliary successor can join distant parts of a sparse graph and disrupt the local structure used by model-checking algorithms. Invariance allows that successor to be chosen freely, but it is unknown whether this freedom always preserves tractability throughout the nowhere dense frontier.',
 source_formulation=dict(text='The 2016 source asks to close the tractability gap with ordinary first-order logic and suggests intermediate sparse graph classes. Bounded expansion was settled in 2017. On 18 September 2026 the user explicitly selected the stronger remaining target on every effectively nowhere dense graph class, parameterized by sentence length.',caption='Eickmeyer–Kawarabayashi, CSL 2016 §6; later bounded-expansion theorem and user-confirmed scope.',citation='primary',format='editorial_paraphrase'),
 references=[ref('primary','Successor-Invariant First-Order Logic on Graphs with Excluded Topological Subgraphs','Kord Eickmeyer; Ken-ichi Kawarabayashi',2016,'https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2016.18','§6, p.18:13'),ref('expansion','Model-Checking for Successor-Invariant First-Order Formulas on Graph Classes of Bounded Expansion','Jan van den Heuvel; Stephan Kreutzer; Michał Pilipczuk; Daniel A. Quiroz; Roman Rabinovich; Sebastian Siebertz',2017,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.08516','Main theorem and computability caveat; author manuscript dated 20 March 2018, §3 Theorem 3'),ref('fo','Deciding First-Order Properties of Nowhere Dense Graphs','Martin Grohe; Stephan Kreutzer; Sebastian Siebertz',2017,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.3899','Main model-checking theorem; JACM 64(3), article 17')],
 context_blocks=[block('The source separates successor invariance from the stronger access to an entire linear order. This card concerns only the successor relation.'),block('The bounded-expansion result covers earlier excluded-minor and excluded-topological-minor milestones. Its explicit effectiveness caveat motivates requiring a computable sparsity bound in the present target.','expansion'),block('Ordinary first-order model checking is fixed-parameter tractable on effectively nowhere dense classes. Extending that frontier to the successor-invariant promise is the selected remaining question.','fo')],
 progress=[progress('2016','The source proves tractability for excluded-topological-subgraph classes and asks for broader sparse classes.'),progress('2017','Successor-invariant model checking reaches bounded expansion, with computable parameter dependence for effective classes.','expansion'),progress('2026-09-18','The user selected the effectively nowhere dense frontier, keeping the already settled bounded-expansion milestone as context.','expansion')],
),['Applied the user-selected effectively nowhere dense graph frontier.','Defined shallow minors, effective sparsity, finite graph logic and global successor invariance.','Separated the promise of invariance from the undecidable task of recognizing invariant formulas.','Recorded the known bounded-expansion result and assessed importance individually at 85.'],['Read the 2016 primary source §6 in context, separating successor and order invariance.','Read the 2017 theorem in the author manuscript dated March 2018, including the directed-path definition and computable-function caveat.','Checked the primary Grohe–Kreutzer–Siebertz model-checking statement and bounded later searches through 18 September 2026; no verified extension settling the selected frontier was found.'],'Source-open as the broader tractability frontier; the bounded-expansion milestone was settled in 2017. The user selected the remaining effectively nowhere dense graph target on 18 September 2026. Bounded primary-source and later-work checks found no verified resolution, without claiming exhaustive literature coverage.',summary=['A first-order sentence may use a successor path through all vertices, provided its answer is independent of the chosen path.','The question asks for fixed-parameter model checking on every effectively nowhere dense graph class.','The parameter is the sentence length, and the polynomial exponent in the graph input must not depend on it.','The algorithm receives promises of class membership and successor invariance rather than having to verify them.','The bounded-expansion case is known, while the selected target reaches the broader sparse-graph frontier of ordinary first-order logic.'],expected_sha256=claim['input_sha256'],claim_token=claim['token'])
